    {
      "n": 4,
      "title": "Protein Structure & the Central Dogma",
      "competency": "STEM_BIO11/12-IIIa-b-4",
      "content": "<h3>🎯 Learning Objectives</h3><ul><li>Describe protein structure.</li><li>Explain the central dogma of molecular biology.</li><li>Define transcription and translation.</li><li>Relate DNA to protein function.</li></ul><h3>🔑 Key Concepts</h3><ul><li><strong>Central dogma</strong> — DNA → RNA → Protein.</li><li><strong>Transcription</strong> — DNA → mRNA.</li><li><strong>Translation</strong> — mRNA → protein.</li><li><strong>Codon</strong> — 3 nucleotides that code for an amino acid.</li></ul><h3>📚 Lesson Content</h3><h4>A. The Central Dogma</h4><p>DNA → RNA → Protein. Genetic information flows from DNA to RNA to protein.</p><h4>B. Transcription</h4><p>Occurs in the nucleus. RNA polymerase makes mRNA from a DNA template.</p><h4>C. Translation</h4><p>Occurs at the ribosome. mRNA codons are matched to tRNA anticodons, adding amino acids to a growing polypeptide.</p><h4>D. Protein Structure</h4><ul><li>Primary — amino acid sequence</li><li>Secondary — alpha helices, beta sheets</li><li>Tertiary — 3D folding</li><li>Quaternary — multiple chains</li></ul>",
      "activity1": {
